<!-- BEGIN baseline:dotnet-conventions v0.7.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: .NET Conventions

- Use `Add*`, not `TryAdd*`, when registering every implementation of a multi-implementation interface (one resolved as `IEnumerable<T>`, e.g. a strategy pattern) — `TryAdd*` dedups by service type alone and silently no-ops a second implementation, with no error.
- Don't bake a per-call value (e.g. a host resolved from a DB row) into a pooled/shared DI instance's registration — a typed `HttpClient` (`AddHttpClient<T>()`) assumes a static base URL known at registration time; build the full URI per call instead of setting `BaseAddress` at registration when the real host varies per call. The same trap applies to a `DelegatingHandler` registered via `AddHttpMessageHandler<T>()` — it's pooled, not per-request, so per-request state must flow through `HttpRequestMessage.Options` (or headers), never a constructor-injected scoped service.
- Treat an unexpected scaffold diff (EF Core migration or any codegen tool producing changes beyond the intended edit) as evidence of pre-existing model/config drift, not something you caused. Verify with a throwaway empty re-scaffold; if it reproduces the same diff, fix the root config, never hand-edit the generated output.
- Implement a cross-cutting concern (audit, retry, validation) as a handler registered into an existing pipeline seam, not an opt-in helper method call sites must remember to invoke.
- Place a cross-cutting `DelegatingHandler` relative to a retry handler deliberately — inside retry observes physical attempts, outside retry observes logical calls.
- When chaining into a different library's fluent builder mid-pipeline, don't cast its return type back — call it as a standalone statement and return your own original reference.
- A fire-and-forget async operation must own its own DI scope, its own `CancellationToken.None`, and its own exception handling — never inherit any of the three from its trigger.
- A nullable foreign-key column meaning "no associated record" must receive a real `null` end-to-end, never a sentinel or randomly-generated value.
- In a migration's raw `InsertData`/`DeleteData`/`UpdateData` column literals, use the schema's original column casing, never the C# entity property's casing.
- Before a Singleton reaches for `IServiceScopeFactory.CreateScope()` to get a fresh handle to a shorter-lived resource, check for a purpose-built factory for that resource type first (e.g. `IHttpClientFactory`), and use manual scope creation only when none exists.
- Register a `DelegatingHandler` shared across more than one typed `HttpClient` via `AddHttpMessageHandler<T>()` as Transient, never Singleton.
- When two options classes bind the same config section, treat one reading a property from the other instead of owning its own as a design smell to flag during implementation.
- Before adding a generic type parameter to accept "one of several caller types" while preserving encapsulation, check whether the constraint interface it requires already does the whole job — skip the generic unless the concrete type must flow back out past the method's boundary.
- Before adding a method to a shared strategy-pattern interface, check whether sibling implementers treat existing methods as genuine capabilities or as permanent unfillable stubs — a method intrinsically tied to one implementer's protocol belongs on a narrow capability interface instead of the shared one, justified by demonstrated (not hypothetical) risk.
- Before naming a new shared abstraction merged from separate call sites, check the domain model's existing vocabulary (status fields, enums, doc comments) for a narrower established meaning tied to only one of the call sites — prefer a mechanical/structural name over a state-implying one when call sites don't uniformly share that state.
- An idempotency/replay short-circuit that finds a record by key must separately validate the record's current state (status/expiry) before reusing it in a response — existence at that key is not the same as still being valid to reuse.
- When auditing every call-site of a conditional credential-routing system (e.g. multiple HttpClient configurations), enumerate every method x its actual routing rule from the code itself (not memory) into a full table separating "by-design", "correctly exempted", and "should-be-exempted-but-isn't" — don't spot-check.
- When writing/updating a parent and its child in one operation, check the ORM model for an existing navigation property before doing a manual second save — attach the child to the parent's navigation property before the first save so the change tracker resolves the foreign key in one call.
- Before adding an operation to a versioned/public SDK/API interface, confirm a real consumer exists — symmetry or completeness alone is not justification, and new work should extend the live interface, not a deprecated parallel one.
- Don't serialize a value into a shared/generic field only to immediately deserialize it back within the next step of the same operation — pass a typed object/property directly; reserve serialization for genuine cross-boundary handoffs (cross-process, cross-time, external API).
- When adding a field to a widely-implemented shared interface, a green build is the minimum bar, not sufficient proof: separately verify (1) every concrete implementation was actually updated, (2) a reflection-based test-data generator targeting the bare interface won't fail at runtime for unregistered concrete types, and (3) if the new field carries sensitive data, it needs its own masking/redaction tag — it does not inherit protection from wherever it was previously nested.
- A shared "sync whole object graph" update method must not treat a missing/null collection on the input object as "clear this in the DB" when narrow-purpose callers pass partial objects — narrow callers should re-fetch the full object and mutate only their own field, not hand a partial object to a full-sync method.
- Before attaching a shared request/response logging middleware to an HttpClient carrying secrets in the body (e.g. an OAuth client-credentials form POST), verify its default masking scope explicitly covers body content, not just header names — many masking libraries default to header-only redaction.
- A migration rollback can only undo migrations whose Down()/reverse code is compiled into the branch you run it from — if a shared environment's migration history shows entries beyond your current branch, locate the branch that actually owns those extra migrations (search all branches) and roll back from there.
- On Windows, when `dotnet build`/`test` fails with file-copy/file-in-use errors (MSB3026, MSB3027) on a project's own output DLL, check for another running process holding that binary (an IDE debug session, a leftover `dotnet run`) before treating it as a code bug.
- Carry an unconfirmed candidate value alongside an entity (a wrapper record), not as a mutable property on it, when the value must survive an EF Core persist call that returns a fresh re-fetched instance — a mutable property needs an explicit restoration step after every persist call, and misses silently vanish the value.
- Before adding a fetch inside a shared helper, check whether every caller already has the value in scope from an earlier step and thread it through as a parameter instead of re-deriving it.
- After adding a member to a widely-mocked interface, explicitly configure every test double for it — loose mocks (Moq/FakeItEasy defaults) silently default value-type members (`bool`, `int`, enums) with no exception, unlike `Task`-returning members. A green build doesn't prove the doubles are wired correctly.
- When a transitive dependency forces a NuGet version bump and trips `NU1605`, grep the solution for that package's existing pinned version and match the prevailing pin — don't pick the bare minimum that merely satisfies the error.
- Prefer a required property on a shared base interface over an empty marker interface for per-type policy that every implementer must declare — marker interfaces carry no compiler-enforced contract and scatter `is`/`as` checks across call sites.
- Match the codebase's existing selective XML-doc convention (structured docs on public API where the contract isn't obvious, plain `//` on private implementation) rather than converting everything or nothing.
- Build and run throwaway .NET projects under a short path (e.g. `C:\tmp\<short-name>`), not a deep harness-generated scratchpad. This covers **any executable the SDK launches**, not just the app: `dotnet run` failing to start the app's own exe and `dotnet test` failing to start `testhost.exe` (`Win32Exception (267)`) are the same Windows `MAX_PATH` (~260 chars) root cause with different error text, and build/restore succeeding first makes it read as a code problem. Recurred a third time on 2026-09-09 because the shipped wording said "running the app".
- Suspect `DefaultAzureCredential`'s credential-probing chain for unexplained multi-second cold-start latency before blaming DB/HTTP/business logic, especially on a non-Azure-hosted dev machine.
- After an EF Core model/entity-config change, grep the scaffolded `ModelSnapshot.cs` for the expected new DDL to confirm it was actually captured — don't trust "build succeeded." Then prove zero remaining drift with a throwaway empty migration add→remove cycle, and verify boundary behavior against a disposable local DB with raw `sqlcmd`.
- To resolve a NuGet package's exact public API without a decompiler: read its `.nuspec` for the GitHub repo URL, then `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/{path}` to fetch and grep the real source — faster than extracting strings from the binary, for open-source packages only.
- Before trusting a "will/won't retry" claim, check the HTTP-client-level resilience policy (e.g. Polly) registration and its per-endpoint scoping explicitly — a message-bus retry loop and an HTTP-client resilience policy are two independent layers that can silently override or exclude each other, and neither an agent's sweep nor a stated belief substitutes for reading the actual policy registration code.
- XML doc comments apply to public test classes and methods too: the class-level `<summary>` should say what the suite proves and why it is separate from the baseline tests, and any method with non-trivial reasoning takes one as well. A test class is `public` for runner discovery rather than for consumption, so the "genuine public API surface" wording reads as not-applicable and has missed on new test files twice — the reader here is a maintainer skimming for which safety properties are covered, which is the same job.
- Verify a NuGet version copied from template or skill reference code before adding it — those pins are prose, never compiled or CI-checked, and they go stale silently. Query `https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/{package-id-lowercase}/index.json`, or read the `dotnet add package` diagnostics: a non-existent version fails loudly (`NU1103`) but a stale-and-vulnerable one succeeds and surfaces only as an `NU1902`/`NU1903` warning. Doesn't apply to pins in a maintained repo's own `.csproj`.

Apply this baseline before ordinary .NET DI and codegen habits, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions.
<!-- END baseline:dotnet-conventions -->
